If $U=\dot\gamma_{d,w}$, $g_d(X,X)=1$, and $g_d(U,X)=0$, then $$\label{cat:tidal-bound}
 |\operatorname{Riem}(U,X,U,X)|
 \leq (1+2h(w,w))^2 Q_d(w,\tau).$$ The factor is uniformly bounded for data and seeds near a fixed pair.

The outer-horizon generators are a strict radial source for the null ray flow parameterized by increasing regular time $t$ on spatial cosphere directions. More precisely, let $H$ be the horizon Killing generator, normalized by its Killing parameter, so that $$\nabla_HH=\kappa_+H,\qquad H^t>0.$$ There is a smooth positive degree-one frequency scale $b_1$, equal to $|\rho|$ on $k=\rho H^\flat$, such that, for the increasing-time ray field $V$, $$V\log b_1=-\frac{\kappa_+}{H^t}
       \quad\hbox{on the generators}.
 \label{geo:source-frequency}$$ On null directions near the generators, the degree-zero function $$f_H=\frac{E^2+D}{b_1^2}
 \label{geo:source-function}$$ vanishes precisely at the generators and satisfies $Vf_H\geq c_H f_H$ for some $c_H>0$ after shrinking the neighborhood.

Near the energy-normalized trapped set on either temporal sheet, the critical radius $r_c=r_c(L_z/E)$ is smooth. There are smooth homogeneous degree-zero functions $$\begin{aligned}
 z_1&=\frac{\Sigma_K\dot r}{E\sqrt\Delta},\\
 z_2&=\operatorname{sgn}(r-r_c)
       \sqrt{\frac{F(r)^2}{\Delta(r)}
                  -\frac{F(r_c)^2}{\Delta(r_c)}}
 \end{aligned}
 \label{geo:normal-variables}$$ whose common zero set is trapping. For the increasing-time ray field, $$Vz_2=\lambda z_1,\qquad Vz_1=\lambda z_2,
       \qquad \lambda>0.
 \label{geo:normal-system}$$ Consequently $\varphi_\pm=z_1\pm z_2$ satisfy $V\varphi_\pm=\pm\lambda\varphi_\pm$. Their reduced spatial symplectic bracket on either halfwave is nonzero on the trapped set. The transverse rates have a uniform positive lower bound on each fixed normalized trapped set.

Linearizing the joint reduced equation at zero parameters and $g=b$ gives $$\label{st:joint-linearization}
 Lh+Q(\partial_t)a.$$ For $$K_d(y,\zeta)=\int k_0(\sigma)e^{-\zeta\sigma d(y)}\,d\sigma$$ the seven columns of $Q$ are $$\label{st:Q-definition}
 \begin{split}
 Y_m&=H K_d,\\
 Y_v&=(J+\zeta H^1)K_d
                   +2(V-e)^\flat\odot d_yK_d,\\
 Q(\zeta)&=
   \left(\frac{L(\zeta)Y_m}{\zeta},
         \frac{L(\zeta)Y_v}{\zeta^2}\right).
 \end{split}$$ The apparent poles are removable. On every fixed compact set, $Q(\partial_t)$ has only bounded-length causal memories, including distributional derivatives, and its fixed spatial derivative costs are polynomial in frequency. For every fixed $N$, $$\label{st:Q-tail}
 Q(\zeta)=O_N\bigl(r_y^{-2}(1+|\zeta|r_y)^{-N}\bigr),
 \qquad r_y=|y|,\quad \operatorname{Re}\zeta\geq0,$$ with the corresponding symbol derivatives near infinity.

Fix a compact test support and a finite number of required angular derivatives. There are a sufficiently high fixed test Sobolev order $q_*$ and a finite polynomial degree $N_*$ with the following property. Choose the filter order $P$ sufficiently large and $\sigma_0>0$ sufficiently small, then take $\Lambda,R$ sufficiently large in the order specified below. For $0<\operatorname{Re}\zeta\leq D_*$ on that strip and a compactly supported test $f$ of order $q_*$, the spatial transpose equation $$M_R(\zeta)^t v_*=f$$ has a solution satisfying $$\begin{aligned}
 \|v_*\|_{\dot H^{-4}(K)}
 &\leq C R^{3\iota}\langle\zeta\rangle^{N_*}
                       \|f\|_{H^{q_*}},
 \label{st:test-compact}\\
 \|(v_*,r_y(\partial_{r_y}+\zeta)v_*)\|_{L^2_\omega}
 &\leq C r_y^{-1+\iota}R^{3\iota}
                \langle\zeta\rangle^{N_*}\|f\|_{H^{q_*}}
       \qquad(r_y\gg1).
 \label{st:test-radiation}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $K$ is any prescribed fixed compact reach, and the second bound includes the prescribed angular derivatives. The bounds are uniform down to $\operatorname{Re}\zeta\downarrow0$ on a fixed strip reaching a forward Laplace line. The slack $\iota>0$ can be arbitrarily small without making $N_*$ diverge. The associated tested operators are retarded.

Under the simultaneous finite-slab budgets above, there is $\kappa_0>0$ such that, after choosing $R$ and then $T_0$ sufficiently large, $$\label{co2:low-bound}
 \mathsf l\le C R^{-\kappa_0}(\mathsf K_R+\mathsf F)
       +C_{\rm fix}(e_{\rm in}+\mathsf N^2).$$ The exponent can be fixed before enlarging any prior compact reach. Such enlargements change constants but not the available radius gain.

On any fixed enlarged compact reach, the simultaneous budgets imply $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathsf S_a&\le C(R_0)\mathsf K_R+C\mathsf F
                   +C_{\rm fix}(e_{\rm in}+\mathsf N^2),
       &&C\text{ independent of }R_0,\label{co2:sliced-acceleration}\\
 \|g_{\rm co}-\overline G_{p,c}\|_{L^2_{A_S}H^8_{\rm loc}}
   &\le C_{\rm loc}(\mathsf K_R+\mathsf F+\mathsf S_a)
                   +C_{\rm fix}(e_{\rm in}+\mathsf N^2).
                    \label{co2:sliced-static}
\end{aligned}$$ The second estimate includes mixed jets of total order eight, and one extra time derivative with the same mixed order on fixed sets outside the physical trapping neighborhood. This extra derivative will supply the mixed time-radial trace in <a href="#in:trace" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="in:trace">[in:trace]</a> when the exterior solution is restricted to the interior entry cylinder. Constants on these fixed reaches are independent of the upper radius $R$.

Under the simultaneous finite-slab budgets, for some $\kappa>0$, $$\label{co2:high-bound}
 \mathsf K_R\le C R^{-\kappa}(\mathsf F+\mathsf K_R)
       +C_{\rm fix}\bigl(e_{\rm in}+\mathsf N^{3/2}
                                 +(e_{\rm in}\mathsf N)^{1/2}\bigr).$$ The acceleration and sliced static bounds enter this estimate only as coefficient smallness and nonlinear errors, not as additional linear forcing terms. The choices are the stationary energy parameters, then a sufficiently large $R$, then the plateau $T_0$, and finally the bootstrap and data smallness.

For the arbitrary fixed subextremal Kerr family constructed above, the lab target satisfies $$\label{far:target-symbols}
 \begin{gathered}
 B-\eta=O(r^{-1}),\qquad DB=O(r^{-1}\widetilde a),\qquad
 \partial^ds_s=O(r^{1-d}\widetilde a)\quad(d\ge1),\\
 \Theta=O(r^{-1}\widetilde a),\qquad
 f_B=O(r^{-2}\widetilde a),\qquad Df_B=O(r^{-3}\widetilde a),\\
 \qquad r\widetilde a\lesssim\mathsf N\tau_f^{-A/2}.
 \end{gathered}$$ These are symbol estimates at every fixed order needed here, with one inverse power per additional lab derivative. Their constants may depend on the fixed family but not on either matching radius. The equation is $$\label{far:wave-structure}
 \begin{gathered}
 g^{ij}\partial_{ij}h=f_h,\\
 \begin{aligned}
 f_h={}&f_B+O(r^{-2}\partial h+r^{-3}h)
             +Q_\eta(\partial h,\partial h)\\
       &+O((r^{-1}+|h|)|\partial h|^2),
 \end{aligned}
 \end{gathered}$$ up to fixed harmless changes in $f_B$. The first- and zeroth-order background coefficients have a relative $O(\widetilde a)$ gain under $D$. The flat linear wave-gauge contraction of $h$ is $$\label{far:flat-gauge}
 O((r^{-1}+|h|)\partial h)+O(r^{-2}h)
                              +O(r^{-1}\widetilde a).$$ The estimates allow smooth composition in the fields and distributed product differentiation. The quadratic form $Q_\eta$ has the constrained weak-null polarization described below.

After giving the finitely many commuted energies fixed hierarchy weights, there is $c_1>0$ such that $$\label{far:far-bound}
 \mathsf F\le o_{R_0}(1)(\mathsf F+\mathsf S_a)
       +C(R_0)\mathsf K_R
       +C_{\mathrm{fix}}\bigl(\text{input errors}
                                      +\mathsf N^{1+c_1}\bigr).$$ The coefficient denoted by $o_{R_0}(1)$ tends to zero as $R_0\to\infty$, independently of the upper compact matching radius $R$. The input errors vanish with $e_{\mathrm{in}}$, uniformly on bounded small bootstrap ranges.

For physical Kerr parameters in a sufficiently small compact neighborhood of the fixed subextremal parameters, there are exact profiles in the gauge <a href="#in:gauge" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="in:gauge">[in:gauge]</a>, depending smoothly only on $(q,\theta)$ and the parameters and invariant under axial rotations, such that $A,\gamma$ and their inverses extend smoothly to $q=0$, $b=O(q)$, and $$\label{in:profile-orders}
 \chi_i,\ \ell_i+\kappa,\ \beta_i=O(q),\qquad
 \xi_i,\ K,\ c_i^{\mathrm{ang}},\ \mu_i=O(1).$$ These estimates hold after every fixed angular or physical-parameter derivative. Their constants can be fixed before $q_0$ is chosen. The same statement holds with either optical direction chosen to have zero angular shift.

Start actual eikonal coordinates on the prepared cylinder with $y_0=-x$, $y_1=x$, and transport its prepared angular labels along $L_1$. For sufficiently small data in the common base neighborhood, the sum of unit-$x$-cell supremum norms of the differences from frozen profiles is at most an arbitrarily prescribed small number $e_{\mathcal S}$ for $$\label{in:entry-orders}
\begin{array}{ll}
 A,\log A,\gamma,b,\chi_i,\ell_i,\xi_i
     &\text{in angular }H^6,\\
 \gamma&\text{in angular }H^7,\\
 \partial_x(\chi_i,\ell_i,\xi_i,b)|_{\mathcal S}
     &\text{in angular }H^2.
\end{array}$$ Here $\partial_x$ is tangent to the cylinder at fixed prepared angle, and the comparison in the last line uses frozen differentiation. The sum of cell suprema of $\sigma_0$ is also at most $C e_{\mathcal S}$. At each fixed higher order, these initial quantities and their parameter jets on $|x|\le CT$ are bounded by $e^{o(T)}$ for every fixed $C>0$.*

*The low smallness depends only on the common base-order neighborhood; constants may depend on the already fixed $q_0$ and coordinate choices. Higher-order constants may depend on the individual smooth datum.

For a future unit timelike geodesic which is inextendible in $\mathcal P_d$ and never crosses $\mathcal S$, every portion $[t_1,t_2]$ inside a fixed sufficiently large co-moving radius satisfies $$\label{cmp:compact-speed}
 \dot t(t)\leq C\dot t(t_1),\qquad t_1\leq t\leq t_2,$$ with $C$ independent of its duration and of the number of visits to the outer-horizon strip. If the portion starts and ends at an interface where $T_{\mathrm{co}}$ is uniformly future timelike, then $$\label{cmp:return-energy}
 E(t_2)\leq E(t_1)
       \exp\left(C\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\rho_c(t)\,dt\right).$$ In particular, there is no fixed multiplicative loss at each return.

Consider a future unit timelike geodesic starting at a fixed point of the double-null wedge, and put $Y_i=e^{-\kappa y_i}$. Its maximal remaining wedge track has finite proper duration, has an angular limit, and ends in a branch or corner. Each $|\dot Y_i|$ is comparable above and below to its positive initial value, and its screen velocity is bounded on that fixed track. Moreover there is $\delta_c(y_0^{\mathrm{in}},y_1^{\mathrm{in}})$, independent of the starting unit timelike tangent, with $\delta_c\to0$ as both initial coordinates tend to $+\infty$, for which $$\label{cmp:ratio-control}
 e^{-2\delta_c}
       \frac{|\dot Y_0^{\mathrm{in}}|}{|\dot Y_1^{\mathrm{in}}|}
 \leq\frac{|\dot Y_0|}{|\dot Y_1|}
 \leq e^{2\delta_c}
       \frac{|\dot Y_0^{\mathrm{in}}|}{|\dot Y_1^{\mathrm{in}}|}.$$ If the track ends at the corner, then necessarily $$\label{cmp:corner-necessary}
 e^{-2\delta_c}
       \frac{|\dot Y_0^{\mathrm{in}}|}{|\dot Y_1^{\mathrm{in}}|}
 \leq\frac{Y_0^{\mathrm{in}}}{Y_1^{\mathrm{in}}}
 \leq e^{2\delta_c}
       \frac{|\dot Y_0^{\mathrm{in}}|}{|\dot Y_1^{\mathrm{in}}|}.$$ For a general fixed starting point the same conclusions hold with a finite, not necessarily small, logarithmic-variation constant.

Fix a mixed Sobolev comparison order $k\ge10$. For every sufficiently small fixed $\delta_2>0$, one may fix $\varepsilon_0>0$ and then $\nu_0>0$ sufficiently small as follows. Suppose the exact reduced-equation Cauchy jets on $s=0$ satisfy the vacuum constraints and wave-map gauge, agree with $g$ for $v<(1-\nu_0)T$, and differ from the jets of $g+h^p$ by at most $$e^{-7\kappa T/2}
 \quad\hbox{in }H^{k+1}\times H^k(dx\,d\theta).
 \label{pk2:entry-accuracy}$$ Then these data have an exact vacuum development $g'$ on <a href="#pk2:domain" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="pk2:domain">[pk2:domain]</a>, with normalized error $F=g'-g-h^p$ bounded in the through-$k$ mixed energies by $$C e^{(\kappa+\delta_2)T}
 \left(e^{-7\kappa T/2}+A_T\lambda_p^{-M_1}
                         +A_T^2\lambda_p^{P_*}\right).
 \tag{P7}\label{pk2:deep-error}$$ The conclusion holds when this bound has a fixed exponential margin below $A_T$ and the required base packet norms are sufficiently exponentially small. The same estimate, with fixed buffers, controls normalized $C^2$ error.

For every fixed base-order buffer $N_b$, every prescribed inverse accuracy $M_1$, and sufficiently high finite phase and amplitude orders, there is a real exterior perturbation $h^B$ such that $$\begin{split}
 \|h^B\|_{C^{N_b}}&\le A_T\lambda_p^{N_b+3/2}e^{o(T)},\\
 \text{reduced-wave and gauge residuals}
   &\le e^{o(T)}
       \left(A_T\lambda_p^{-M_1}+A_T^2\lambda_p^{P_0}\right),
 \qquad P_0=2N_b+7,
 \end{split}
 \tag{E2}\label{pk2:beam-estimates}$$ after enlarging $N_b$ for the required trace and derivative buffers. Its jets match those of $h^p$ at $\mathcal S$ with linear error $A_T\lambda_p^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}$. The positive exponent $P_0$ depends only on the base orders, not on later formal accuracy. On the original initial slice its support lies in <a href="#pk2:arrival-shell" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="pk2:arrival-shell">[pk2:arrival-shell]</a>, up to arbitrarily thin tube margins.

Fix an integer $s\ge3$. Suppose $d_B=(\delta+a_B,b_B)$ are smooth data on $\mathbb R^3$, the metric is positive, and all derivatives of $a_B,b_B$ have symbol orders $-1,-2$, respectively. Suppose a smooth compact source $\phi$, supported in $\{|y'|\ge1\}$, agrees with $\Phi(d_B)$ there. If $$\|a_B\|_{H_1^{s+2}}+\|b_B\|_{H_2^{s+1}}
       +\|\phi\|_{H_4^s}$$ is sufficiently small, there is a smooth correction $v_c$ supported in $\{|y'|\ge1\}$ such that $$\Phi(d_B+v_c)=0\quad\text{there},
 \qquad \|v_c\|_{X^s}\le C_s\|\phi\|_{H_4^s}.$$ The correction is smooth across the support boundary, preserves positivity, and satisfies symbol bounds at every order. There is no higher-order smallness assumption and no condition on the artificial interior defect. No moment condition is imposed on $\phi$; the correction may have a noncompact tail changing the asymptotic coefficients.

For any prescribed small $\delta_3>0$, the localization parameters and far threshold can be chosen so that the exact ordinary wave-map solution exists on this finite exterior domain and its error from $g+h^B$, in the required fixed mixed norms, is bounded by $$C e^{\delta_3T}
       \left(A_T\lambda_p^{-M_1}+A_T^2\lambda_p^{P_0}\right).
 \tag{E3}\label{pk2:exterior-error}$$ It is exactly $g$ on the earlier needed compact-in-radius region up to $t_T-\alpha'T$, where $\alpha'>0$ can be prescribed sufficiently small. In particular, with the choices below, its entry jets agree exactly with $g$ for $v<(1-\nu_0)T$ and otherwise meet <a href="#pk2:entry-accuracy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="pk2:entry-accuracy">[pk2:entry-accuracy]</a>.

For $r\asymp R_f,\ q\le-R_f^\theta/10$, $\theta=.45$, the center is zero. On a band $-q\asymp Q$, we have $$\label{fe:F6}
\|Z^I(rT^j h)\|_{L^2_\omega}\lesssim (e_{\rm in}+\mathsf S_a)(r/Q)^{1/2}
               \quad (|I|+j\le9),$$ allowing fixed input constants; the constant here on $\mathsf S_a$ is uniform far out. Also the ordinary unweighted $L^2_z$ energy on such a band at fixed $t$ of Minkowski commutations through nine is $\lesssim (e_{\rm in}+\mathsf S_a)Q^{-1/2}$.

The finite-time norms <a href="#fe:F1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fe:F1">[fe:F1]</a> imply $$\label{fe:F7}
\begin{array}{ll}
 \|\chi(h)\|_{L^2_\omega}\lesssim N r^{2\eta_*}\tau^{-A_H/2}
                          & (n_*\le8),\\
 \|\chi(h)\|_{L^2_\omega}\lesssim N r^{.52}
                          & (n_*\le9),\\
 \|B_1\chi(h)\|_{L^2_\omega}\lesssim N r^{e/2}\mu^{-A_H/2}
                          & (n_*\le7).
 \end{array}$$

In any bound with a $T^j$ on an upper-index tensor, contract with $\ell\ell$ *after* those derivatives and then apply the indicated $Z$’s to the full contraction. $$\label{fe:F9}
\begin{array}{ll}
 \|Z^I H_{\ell\ell}\|_{L^2_\omega}
    \lesssim r^{-1}+N r^{-1+2\eta_*}\tau^{-A_H/2}&(|I|\le8),\\
 \|Z^I (T^j H)_{\ell\ell}\|_{L^2_\omega}
    \lesssim r^{-2}+N r^{-2+8\eta_*}\tau^{-A_H/2}
                                 &(j\ge1,\ |I|+j\le8),\\
 \|\partial_q Z^I (T^j H)_{\ell\ell}\|_{L^2_\omega}
    \lesssim r^{-2}+N r^{-2+8\eta_*}\tau^{-A_H/2}
                                 &(|I|+j\le7).
 \end{array}$$

Set $b=1+\min(|q|,r)$. Then $$\label{fe:F10}
\begin{aligned}
    r\|Z^I H_{\ell\ell}\|_{L^2_\omega} &\lesssim 1
                   &&(|I|\le8,\ |q|\le 10 r^\theta),\\
    r\|Z^I (T^j H)_{\ell\ell}\|_{L^2_\omega} &\lesssim r^{-.46}
                   &&(j\ge1,\ |I|+j\le9),\\
    r\|Z^9 H_{\ell\ell}\|_{L^2_\omega}
       &\lesssim \log r + b^{1/2}r^{-p_0/2}+b r^{-1+8\eta_*}.
 \end{aligned}$$ Constants here can be bounded independently of $R_0$ by choosing smallness of the nonlinear quantities after the radii; equivalently retain background plus $O(C_{\rm fix}\mathsf N)$ in them.

Assume that each equation has the splitting <a href="#fe:F13" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fe:F13">[fe:F13]</a>, and that its correction, raw, remainder, and mixed budgets satisfy, in the triangular order specified below, bounds of the form $$\label{fe:source-hypothesis}
o_{R_0\to\infty}(1)(\mathsf F+\mathsf S_a)
  +C\sum_{\rm prior}\mathsf F_{\rm prior}
  +C(R_0)\mathsf K_R+C_{\rm fix}(e_{\rm in}+\mathsf N^{1+c_1})$$ for fixed $c_1>0$. Assume also that the corrections have initial weighted energy and averaged starting-annulus costs bounded by the initial and overlap terms in <a href="#fe:source-hypothesis" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fe:source-hypothesis">[fe:source-hypothesis]</a>; the annular norm is the compact weighted $L^2$ norm. Then the two energy multipliers give $$\label{fe:far-conclusion}
\mathsf F\ \le\ o(1)(\mathsf F+\mathsf S_a)
          +C(R_0)\mathsf K_R+C_{\rm fix}(e_{\rm in}+\mathsf N^{1+c_2})$$ (with $c_2>0$, allowing analogous small input bootstrap errors). It suffices also to take tunably small uniform coefficients on $\mathsf F$ in this argument. A convenient prior order for linear terms is: level $P$ before the $h$ hierarchies, within a level lower total orders first, more lab times first at fixed total order, and then fewer $S$’s first. Small radius gains and perturbative nonlinear effects may cross this order. Norms aggregated at equal indices are treated together.

For each commuted equation on such a finite slab, the sum of its budgets <a href="#fe:F14" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fe:F14">[fe:F14]</a>, together with its mixed charges <a href="#fs:mixed-charge" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fs:mixed-charge">[fs:mixed-charge]</a>, is bounded, for some fixed $c_1>0$, by $$\label{fs:F15}
 o_{R_0\to\infty}(1)(\mathsf F+\mathsf S_a)
 +C\sum_{\mathrm{prior}}\mathsf F_{\mathrm{prior}}
 +C(R_0)\mathsf K_R
 +C_{\mathrm{fix}}(e_{\mathrm{in}}+\mathsf N^{1+c_1}).$$ The finite prior order is: level $P$ before the two $h$ hierarchies; within each level lower total order first, then more laboratory times at equal total order, then fewer dilations $S$. Equal-index norms are aggregated. The constants on prior far quantities are independent of both matching radii. Small radial gains and nonlinear bootstrap products may cross this order. After fixed finite hierarchical weighting, [fe:energy-reduction](#fe:energy-reduction) therefore gives, for some $c_2>0$, $$\label{fs:F}
 \mathsf F\le o_{R_0\to\infty}(1)(\mathsf F+\mathsf S_a)
 +C(R_0)\mathsf K_R
 +C_{\mathrm{fix}}(e_{\mathrm{in}}+\mathsf N^{1+c_2}).$$ The initial and starting-annulus correction costs satisfy the same bound. In particular the pure acceleration defect on the starting annulus has its direct small radial coefficient on $\mathsf S_a$.
